clients/windows/src/app/rules.rs	It names the host by its label or address, which anything that can open a link could guess. Shortcuts made in Punktfunk name the host's id and connect without asking.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/about.rs	Open log folder
clients/windows/src/app/settings/about.rs	The client log (client.log, plus the session’s whole receive/decode/present trail) — attach it to a bug report.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/about.rs	Third-party licenses
clients/windows/src/app/settings/audio.rs	Applies from the next session.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/audio.rs	Host audio plays here — System default follows the Windows output device.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/audio.rs	Keeps the host’s audio, playing from this machine’s speakers, from being picked up and sent straight back. Turn it off if your microphone already does its own processing.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/audio.rs	Lossless sends uncompressed PCM instead of Opus — bit-exact, at 2.3–4.6 Mb/s taken off the top of the link and outside the automatic-bitrate loop. The host has its own switch, off by default, and quietly stays on Opus if it can’t deliver the rate; the stats overlay names what the session actually got.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/audio.rs	System default
clients/windows/src/app/settings/audio.rs	The host’s own speakers or headphones keep playing while you stream — both ends hear the same audio. Needs a host on 0.32 or newer.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/audio.rs	The input that feeds the host’s virtual mic.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/audio.rs	The speaker layout requested from the host. It downmixes if its own output has fewer channels.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/audio.rs	This device’s microphone feeds the host’s virtual mic. Ctrl+Alt+Shift+V mutes and unmutes it during a stream.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/audio.rs	{saved} (not detected)
clients/windows/src/app/settings/controllers.rs	Applies from the next session.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/controllers.rs	Automatic (all controllers)
clients/windows/src/app/settings/controllers.rs	Automatic (match the controller)
clients/windows/src/app/settings/controllers.rs	Detected controllers
clients/windows/src/app/settings/controllers.rs	Every connected controller is forwarded, each as its own player. Pick one to force single-player — only it reaches the host.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/controllers.rs	Hold Select on its own to press the host's guide button — keep holding for a Gaming-Mode host's quick-access menu. A Select tap still goes through, slightly delayed. Automatic arms it only where the real button can't reach the host.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/controllers.rs	No controllers detected
clients/windows/src/app/settings/controllers.rs	Off, controllers don't vibrate from the stream or in the menus, whatever the game sends.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/controllers.rs	Play a DualSense's voice-coil haptics on the pad itself, while controllers are forwarded.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/controllers.rs	Play the audio a game sends to the pad's own speaker on the pad, not through this PC.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/controllers.rs	Plug in or pair a controller and it appears here.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/controllers.rs	Send to host
clients/windows/src/app/settings/controllers.rs	Sends controllers connected to this PC to the host. Turn it off when your controller already reaches the host another way — USB passthrough such as VirtualHere, or a pad plugged into the host itself — so games don't see two of them. Off, this PC never opens the controller at all, which is what leaves it free for a passthrough tool to claim.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/controllers.rs	Steam Controller 2
clients/windows/src/app/settings/controllers.rs	Steam Input's virtual pad — Automatic skips it while a real pad is connected
clients/windows/src/app/settings/controllers.rs	The virtual pad created on the host. Automatic matches your controller — a DualSense keeps adaptive triggers, lightbar, touchpad and motion.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/controllers.rs	This device
clients/windows/src/app/settings/controllers.rs	Where the guide (Xbox/PS) and quick-access presses go while streaming. Automatic sends them to the host — except on devices whose own overlay reacts to the same press (Gaming Mode), where they stay local and the gesture below reaches the host.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	A preference — the host falls back if it can’t encode it. PyroWave is the low-latency wavelet codec for a WIRED link: it trades bitrate (hundreds of Mb/s) for near-zero decode time, so it wants gigabit Ethernet.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	Above native supersamples for sharpness; below renders lighter on the host and the link. This device resamples the result to the window.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	All monitors
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	All monitors opens a fullscreen window on each of this computer's monitors, one host screen each, laid out as your monitors sit. Close a window to give its monitor back.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	Aspect ratio
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	Automatic (GPU, fall back to CPU)
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	Automatic (the display's GPU)
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	Automatic picks the hardware path this GPU does best — Direct3D 11 on Intel, Vulkan Video on NVIDIA and AMD — and falls back to the CPU. Change it only when debugging.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	Crop to fill
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	Display changes apply from the next session.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	Frames held back before showing. Each one absorbs about a refresh of network hiccup and adds a refresh of delay. Automatic holds two.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	Full-colour video: crisp small text and thin lines, at more bandwidth. Requires an NVIDIA host (NVENC) or the PyroWave codec — other encoders stream 4:2:0.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	HDR10, when the host has HDR content and this display supports it. With H.264 the stream stays SDR.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	Hardware (Direct3D 11 / DXVA)
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	Hardware (Vulkan Video)
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	In Mb/s. 0 lets the host decide (its default, clamped to what it supports). A host card’s context menu has a network speed test.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	Lowest latency
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	Lowest latency shows each frame the moment the display can take it — a network hiccup becomes an occasional repeated or skipped frame. Smoothness buffers a little to even those out.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	Match window
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	Native display
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	Off, a stream keeps the screen awake only while you play. Left alone for five minutes, it lets Windows’ own screen saver or sleep take over.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	On a VRR/FreeSync/G-Sync screen, let the panel refresh in step with the stream instead of on a fixed cadence. Applies to fullscreen sessions; harmless on a fixed-refresh screen.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	PyroWave (wired LAN)
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	Smoother gradients without HDR — the picture is encoded at 10-bit precision. Needs an NVIDIA host; HDR takes over when it engages.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	Software (CPU)
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	Stretch to fill
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	Tear-free. Turning it off removes the wait for the screen’s refresh — the lowest possible delay, at the cost of visible tearing. Not every driver offers it; the stats overlay names the mode actually in use.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	The backend the host uses for its virtual output (Linux hosts only). A specific choice falls back to auto-detection when that backend isn’t available.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	The host drives a real virtual output at exactly this size — true pixels, no scaling. “Native display” follows the monitor this window is on; “Match window” keeps the picture pixel-exact (1:1) through every resize.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	This monitor
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	When the stream's shape differs from the window. Fit shows the whole picture with black bars, Crop to fill cuts the edges off, Stretch to fill distorts it.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	Which adapter decodes and presents the stream. Automatic uses the GPU driving this window’s display.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	Which shapes the Resolution list offers. Picking one moves to its size nearest the current height.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/display.rs	“Native” resolves to this display’s refresh rate at connect.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/general.rs	Adds the settings most players never need to change.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/general.rs	Always opens it at launch. Otherwise it opens when a controller connects, and this window returns when the last one disconnects.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/general.rs	Connecting to a saved host that’s offline sends Wake-on-LAN and waits for it to boot. Turn off if hosts behind a VPN look offline when they aren’t.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/general.rs	Go fullscreen when a session starts; F11 or Alt+Enter switches back live.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/general.rs	Library opens {}’s games; Stream also connects to its desktop. Back leaves either one on the host list.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/general.rs	Live session stats in a corner overlay — Compact is a one-line pill, Detailed adds the stage breakdown. Ctrl+Alt+Shift+S cycles the tiers any time.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/general.rs	Off shows the figures Moonlight's overlay also shows. On shows capture to glass as p50/p95 and every stage between.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/general.rs	Opens on the host list: there is no default host yet. Pair one, or pick one from a host’s menu when several are paired.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/general.rs	Opens the console, the interface built for a controller.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/general.rs	Shows how to leave for a few seconds when a stream starts.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/general.rs	The corner the statistics overlay sits in.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/general.rs	The overlay's size, on top of your display's scaling.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/general.rs	What each number means
clients/windows/src/app/settings/general.rs	With a controller
clients/windows/src/app/settings/input.rs	Alt+Tab, the Windows key and friends reach the host while the stream has input captured. Off, they act on this machine instead.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/input.rs	Capture (games)
clients/windows/src/app/settings/input.rs	Capture locks the pointer to the stream and sends relative motion — best for games. Desktop leaves the pointer free to enter and leave the stream and sends absolute positions — best for remote desktop work. Ctrl+Alt+Shift+M switches live.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/input.rs	Desktop (absolute)
clients/windows/src/app/settings/input.rs	Direct pointer
clients/windows/src/app/settings/input.rs	How a touchscreen drives the host: Trackpad moves the host cursor like a laptop trackpad (tap to click), Direct pointer jumps the cursor to wherever you touch, Touch passthrough sends real multi-touch through.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/input.rs	Reverses the wheel and trackpad scroll direction sent to the host.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/input.rs	The dial Ctrl+Alt+Shift+O, a two-finger twist or Select+A opens in a stream: what its six buttons hold, and the shortcut chords they can send. A preset that changes it owns the whole dial.
clients/windows/src/app/settings/input.rs	Touch passthrough
